  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 1,
    "lesson_title": "Fourier Transform of a One-Sided Exponential",
    "lesson_core_move": "Compute the continuous-time Fourier transform of a decaying one-sided exponential e^(-a t)$u(t)$ by applying the transform definition over its step-limited support and identifying the basic pair e^(-a t)$u(t) <-> 1/(a + j \\omega) for a > 0$.",
    "section_count": 4,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Fourier Transform of a One-Sided Exponential",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Fourier Transform of a One-Sided Exponential",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The signal to recognize here is a decaying one-sided exponential, $x(t)=e^{-at}u(t)$ with $a>0$. The unit step $u(t)$ is the cue that the signal is zero for $t<0$ and equals the exponential only on the nonnegative-time side.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(\\omega)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(t)e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use that cue to rewrite the transform over the step-limited support, so the integral runs from $0$ to $\\infty$. At this point you are setting up the transform, not evaluating it yet.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(\\omega)=\\int_0^\\infty e^{-at}e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Combine the two exponentials into the single kernel $e^{-(a+j\\omega)t}$. Because $a>0$, the kernel decays as $t$ grows, which is the convergence condition that makes this one-sided exponential a standard Fourier-transform pair.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{-at}u(t)\\;\\leftrightarrow\\;\\frac{1}{a+j\\omega},\\qquad a>0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That pair is the result to keep in view for the rest of this lesson: one-sided support gives the lower limit, and the exponential decay gives the denominator $a+j\\omega$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Fourier Transform of a One-Sided Exponential",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Setting Up the Transform Integral for a One-Sided Exponential",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Set up the Fourier transform integral for $x(t)=e^{-2t}u(t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the unit step $u(t)$: it turns $x(t)=e^{-2t}u(t)$ into a one-sided signal, so the Fourier-transform setup only needs the nonnegative-time support.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(\\omega)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(t)e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start from the transform definition and use the support limit from $u(t)$ to replace the lower bound with $0$ and the upper bound with $\\infty$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(\\omega)=\\int_0^\\infty e^{-2t}e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Keep the decay factor and the kernel visible long enough to show the product. Then combine them into a single exponential so the setup is ready for the next step.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(\\omega)=\\int_0^\\infty e^{-(2+j\\omega)t}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That is the complete setup for this example: one-sided support sets the limits, and the decay constant becomes part of the complex exponent. The next step can evaluate the integral from this starting point.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Fourier Transform of a One-Sided Exponential",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Evaluating the Exponential Integral",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Evaluate the Fourier transform integral for $x(t)=e^{-2t}u(t)$ and simplify the result.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start from the setup integral for $x(t)=e^{-2t}u(t)$ and treat it as an improper integral, since the upper limit extends to infinity.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(\\omega)=\\lim_{b\\to\\infty}\\int_0^b e^{-(2+j\\omega)t}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the antiderivative of the exponential kernel, keeping the denominator $2+j\\omega$ intact.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(\\omega)=\\lim_{b\\to\\infty}\\left[-\\frac{e^{-(2+j\\omega)t}}{2+j\\omega}\\right]_0^b",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because the real part of $2+j\\omega$ is $2>0$, the exponential term vanishes as $b\\to\\infty$. At $t=0$, the exponential equals $1$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(\\omega)=\\lim_{b\\to\\infty}\\frac{1-e^{-(2+j\\omega)b}}{2+j\\omega}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the closed form is $X(\\omega)=\\frac{1}{2+j\\omega}$. That is the evaluated transform for the one-sided exponential with decay constant $2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Fourier Transform of a One-Sided Exponential",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Writing the One-Sided Exponential Transform Pair",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Write the transform pair for $e^{-at}u(t)$ and use it to identify the transform of $e^{-4t}u(t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The reusable cue is the one-sided exponential $e^{-at}u(t)$ with $a>0$: the unit step limits the signal to $t\\ge 0$, so the Fourier transform can be stored as a lookup pair.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{-at}u(t)\\;\\leftrightarrow\\;\\frac{1}{a+j\\omega},\\qquad a>0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The earlier calculation with $a=2$ produced exactly the same denominator pattern, so it is the concrete check that the lookup rule matches the integral evaluation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{-2t}u(t)\\;\\leftrightarrow\\;\\frac{1}{2+j\\omega}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now use the same template with $a=4$ to read off the transform of $e^{-4t}u(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{-4t}u(t)\\;\\leftrightarrow\\;\\frac{1}{4+j\\omega}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the final lookup form is $e^{-at}u(t)\\leftrightarrow\\frac{1}{a+j\\omega}$ for $a>0$, and any specific positive decay constant drops straight into the denominator as $a+j\\omega$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
